"""Illustrated first-encounter book for the alcohol / carboxylic-acid class."""
from html import escape as E
import re
def formula_html(value):
 saved=[]
 def protect(match):
  saved.append('<span class="fg-acid">'+match[0]+'</span>');return "\uE000"+str(len(saved)-1)+"\uE001"
 text=re.sub(r"[–-]?C(?:\(=O\)[–-]?OH|OOH)",protect,E(str(value)))
 text=re.sub(r"(?<=[0-9₀-₉(–-])OH(?![⁻−-])",'<span class="fg-alcohol">OH</span>',text)
 return re.sub("\uE000(\\d+)\uE001",lambda m:saved[int(m[1])],text)
from organic_class_content import section
ASSET='../../../../../assets/y10-chemistry/alcohols/'
def chapter(title,paragraphs,example,memory,terms,think,answer,picture=None):
 d=section(title,paragraphs,example,memory);d.update(terms=terms,think=think,answer=answer,picture=picture);return d
CHAPTERS=[
 chapter('Learn the language of a molecular drawing',[
 'Start with the smallest pieces of the drawing. An atom is a unit of a chemical element. An element contains one kind of atom, identified by a chemical symbol: C is carbon, H is hydrogen and O is oxygen. A molecule is a group of atoms bonded together. Ethanol is a molecule containing all three of these elements.',
 'A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons. Electrons are negatively charged particles in atoms. One line, – , represents one shared pair; two lines, = , represent a double bond with two shared pairs. In the ordinary neutral molecules shown here, carbon forms four bonds, oxygen two and hydrogen one. A double bond counts as two when checking this total.',
 'A subscript is a small number written after a symbol. In CH₃, the ₃ counts three hydrogen atoms attached to that carbon. In CH₃–CH₂–OH, count each group separately: one carbon plus one carbon, three H plus two H plus the H on oxygen, and one O. The molecule therefore has two carbons, six hydrogens and one oxygen.',
 'A molecular formula, such as C₂H₆O, gives the total atom counts. A structural formula also shows connections. CH₃–CH₂–OH is a condensed structural formula: the groups save space, while the lines show how they join. When you see C(=O), the brackets indicate an oxygen double-bonded to that carbon; they do not mean that a separate oxygen molecule is floating nearby.'
 ],['Read CH₃–CH₂–OH from left to right.','Count C: 1 + 1 = 2. Count H: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. Count O: 1.','Check the second carbon: one C–C bond, two C–H bonds and one C–O bond make four.'],'A subscript counts atoms. A line shows a bond. Connections tell you more than totals alone.',{'atom':'A unit of an element.','molecule':'A bonded group of atoms.','covalent bond':'A shared pair of electrons.','subscript':'A small number after a symbol that counts atoms.'},'Does C₂H₅OH contain five or six hydrogen atoms?','Six. C₂H₅ counts five in the carbon-containing part; the final OH supplies another H.'),
 chapter('Meet the alcohol family',[
 'A functional group is an arrangement of atoms that gives a molecule characteristic chemical reactions. In the simple alcohols here, the group is a bonded –OH attached to a saturated carbon: a carbon connected through single bonds. This –OH is called a hydroxyl group. It is not the free hydroxide ion OH⁻ found in sodium hydroxide.',
 'Look at ethanol, CH₃–CH₂–OH. The oxygen is bonded to a carbon and a hydrogen. Methanol, CH₃–OH, has one carbon. Ethanol has two, and propan-1-ol, CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH, has three. Their different carbon chains share the same alcohol group. The name ending –ol is a useful clue, but the structure is the evidence.',
 'The O–H group helps small alcohol molecules interact with water. Hydrogen bonding is an attraction involving H attached to an O atom and a neighbouring O atom’s electrons. Ethanol and water mix. As the carbon chain becomes longer, the carbon-containing part generally makes these alcohols less soluble in water: soluble means able to dissolve. The hydroxyl group has not disappeared.',
 'In the usual dilute-water classroom comparison, ethanol is approximately neutral. It does not behave like sodium hydroxide simply because both formulas contain O and H. Later, we will meet another group that contains O–H but behaves as an acid. Always read the neighbours of the group.'
 ],['Circle the terminal –OH in CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH.','The carbon attached to O has single bonds and is not a carbonyl carbon.','Identify propan-1-ol as an alcohol; the three-carbon chain changes the name, not the family.'],'For these simple alcohols: –ol in the name, –OH on a saturated carbon in the structure.',{'functional group':'An atom arrangement responsible for characteristic reactions.','hydroxyl group':'A bonded –OH group.','saturated carbon':'A carbon with single bonds to its neighbours.','soluble':'Able to dissolve in the stated solvent.'},'Does making the carbon chain longer automatically remove the alcohol group?','No. If the appropriate –OH remains, the molecule still belongs to the alcohol family.'),
 chapter('Use and make ethanol: everyday chemistry',[
 'A solvent is a substance that dissolves another substance. Ethanol is used as a solvent in formulated products, and it can be part of a fuel blend. These uses depend on its properties; “alcohol” is a chemical family name, not a claim that every alcohol is suitable for drinking. Methanol, for example, is poisonous.',
 'Fermentation is a biological process in which yeast enzymes convert glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide under oxygen-poor conditions. Yeast is a microorganism, and an enzyme is a biological catalyst. A catalyst speeds a reaction without being consumed overall. The simplified equation is C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.',
 'The large numbers in front of formulas are coefficients. They give relative numbers of molecules. The 2 before CO₂ means two complete carbon dioxide molecules, not a new molecule called C₂O₄. Count six carbons, twelve hydrogens and six oxygens on each side of the fermentation equation.',
 'Industry can also make ethanol by reacting ethene with water under suitable catalyst, temperature and pressure conditions: C₂H₄ + H₂O ⇌ C₂H₅OH. Ethene is a hydrocarbon with a C=C bond; adding the elements of water across it can form an alcohol. The ⇌ symbol means that the reaction can proceed in both directions. Fermentation and industrial hydration are different routes to the same ethanol molecule.'
 ],['In the fermentation equation, two ethanol molecules contain four carbons altogether.','Two CO₂ molecules contain the remaining two carbons.','The same six carbon atoms have been rearranged into new molecules; none have disappeared.'],'Fermentation: glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide. Coefficients multiply the whole formula.',{'solvent':'A substance that dissolves another substance.','fermentation':'The yeast-catalysed conversion model described here.','catalyst':'A substance that speeds a reaction without being consumed overall.','coefficient':'A number before a formula that counts complete molecules.'},'Which gas explains the bubbles in the simplified fermentation model?','Carbon dioxide, CO₂. Ethanol is the other named product.','uses'),
 chapter('Meet the complete carboxyl group',[
 'A carboxylic acid contains a carboxyl group: –C(=O)–OH, shortened to –COOH. Focus on one central carbon. It is double-bonded to one oxygen, single-bonded to an O–H group and single-bonded to the rest of the molecule. The C=O part is called a carbonyl group. Carbon still has four bonds: 2 + 1 + 1.',
 'In the drawings below, the double-bonded O points steeply upwards and the OH points downwards. Both are attached to the same carbon. The branches help you see the complete group; changing how a formula is drawn on the page does not create a different compound. The drawings show connectivity rather than a measurement of bond lengths.',
 'Methanoic acid, H–C(=O)–OH, has one carbon. Ethanoic acid, CH₃–C(=O)–OH, has two. Propanoic acid, CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–OH, has three. Count the carboxyl carbon in the total. These simple acid names end in –oic acid.',
 'Compare ethanol with ethanoic acid. Ethanol has an alcohol hydroxyl group on a saturated carbon. Ethanoic acid has the complete –COOH arrangement, including C=O. The OH inside a carboxyl group is not enough to classify it as an alcohol. The neighbouring carbonyl changes the chemical behaviour. Vinegar is a dilute aqueous solution containing ethanoic acid; aqueous means dissolved in water.'
 ],['Find the C=O branch above the central carbon.','Find the C–OH branch below it. Treat them as one –COOH group.','For CH₃–COOH, count the CH₃ carbon and the carboxyl carbon: two in total, so ethanoic acid.'],'O double-bonded up + OH single-bonded down, on the SAME carbon: the whole group is –COOH.',{'carbonyl':'A carbon double-bonded to oxygen, C=O.','carboxyl group':'The complete –C(=O)–OH arrangement.','aqueous':'Dissolved in water.'},'Why is CH₃COOH not classified as an alcohol just because OH appears in its formula?','Its O–H is part of the complete carboxyl group on a carbonyl carbon. Read C=O and O–H together.','acids'),
 chapter('Why “weak acid” and “dilute acid” mean different things',[
 'An acid can donate a proton, H⁺. An ion is an atom or group of atoms with an electrical charge. In water, H⁺ is hydrated: it is associated with water molecules rather than existing as a bare floating proton. School equations often write H⁺ as a convenient shorthand.',
 'Ethanoic acid is a weak acid because only a proportion of its molecules ionise in water. Ionisation means forming ions. A simplified equation is CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺. The ethanoate ion, CH₃COO⁻, has lost the acidic H, while its negative charge is indicated by the raised minus sign. The reversible arrow shows that both directions are possible.',
 'Concentration describes how much substance is present in a given amount or volume of solution. Dilute means a relatively low concentration. Weak describes how extensively an acid ionises under the stated conditions. Adding water dilutes a solution; it does not turn the acid into a different, “strong” acid. Ionisation extent can also depend on concentration, so do not treat a weak acid as having one fixed percentage ionised in every solution.',
 'pH is a measure of acidity. For water at about 25 °C, pH 7 is neutral, lower values indicate acidity and higher values indicate basicity. The pH of a particular acid solution depends on concentration as well as acid behaviour; the word weak does not give a single pH value.'
 ],['Ask “How much acid per solution volume?” to discuss concentration.','Ask “How extensively does it ionise?” to discuss strength.','A vinegar sample can contain a weak acid and also be dilute, but those two descriptions answer different questions.'],'Weak = partial ionisation. Dilute = low concentration. Never swap the two meanings.',{'proton':'H⁺ in these acid–base equations.','ion':'An atom or group with an electrical charge.','ionisation':'Formation of ions.','concentration':'Amount of substance per stated amount or volume of solution.'},'Does “ethanoic acid is weak” mean that it contains no hydrogen?','No. It contains hydrogen; “weak” describes partial ionisation in water, not absence of hydrogen.'),
 chapter('Predict the reactions of a carboxylic acid',[
 'A base reacts with an acid; in the proton model, it can accept H⁺. Neutralisation of ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide forms a salt and water: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O. A salt is an ionic compound made from positive and negative ions. Here the salt is sodium ethanoate, with sodium and ethanoate ions.',
 'Neutralisation names the reaction, not a promise that every mixture will finish at pH 7. The final solution depends on what was mixed and in what amounts. The familiar H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O equation shows proton and hydroxide reacting to form water; it is not a claim that ethanol supplies free OH⁻.',
 'A carbonate or hydrogencarbonate can react with an acid to release carbon dioxide. Sodium hydrogencarbonate, NaHCO₃, is commonly called baking soda. For this example: CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂. The products are a salt, water and carbon dioxide.',
 'Effervescence means visible gas bubbles forming. It is different from boiling. Bubbles alone do not identify a gas; carbon dioxide turns limewater milky in its standard test. In this ordinary comparison, ethanol does not show the same acid–carbonate effervescence as ethanoic acid. The two molecules contain O–H but their full groups behave differently.'
 ],['Identify the acid as ethanoic acid and the hydrogencarbonate as baking soda.','Predict sodium ethanoate + water + carbon dioxide.','Use gas formation as an observation, and an appropriate test to identify the gas; do not call every bubble hydrogen.'],'Acid + carbonate/hydrogencarbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide.',{'base':'A proton acceptor in the model used here.','neutralisation':'An acid–base reaction.','salt':'An ionic compound of positive and negative ions.','effervescence':'Visible formation of gas bubbles.'},'Vinegar and baking soda bubble at room temperature. Does that mean the liquid is boiling?','No. The reaction produces carbon dioxide gas; effervescence is not the same process as boiling.','fizz'),
 chapter('Connect ethanol to ethanoic acid',[
 'Ethanol can be oxidised to ethanoic acid under suitable conditions. For this primary alcohol conversion, oxidation can be described as gaining oxygen and/or losing hydrogen. In ethanol, the –OH-bearing carbon is attached to one other carbon; this is a primary alcohol. Do not assume every alcohol gives the same acid.',
 'The simplified overall equation is CH₃CH₂OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O. Follow the two-carbon skeleton: it stays two carbons long while the alcohol end becomes a carboxyl group. In vinegar production, microorganisms help use oxygen during the conversion of ethanol into ethanoic acid.',
 'Keep fermentation and this oxidation separate. Fermentation converts glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide under oxygen-poor conditions. The vinegar conversion uses oxygen and produces ethanoic acid from ethanol. A reversible arrow in another reaction does not mean these two different processes have identical conditions.',
 'The next class connects alcohols and carboxylic acids in another way: under suitable acid-catalysed conditions they can form an ester and water. An ester is a different functional-group family, studied in the Esters and polyesters class. First become confident distinguishing the alcohol and acid structures here.'
 ],['On the left, ethanol has two carbons, six hydrogens and one oxygen, with another two oxygens supplied by O₂.','On the right, ethanoic acid and water contain two carbons, six hydrogens and three oxygens in total.','Every atom is conserved. The change in connections explains why the product belongs to a different family.'],'Fermentation makes ethanol; oxidation of ethanol can make ethanoic acid.',{'oxidation':'For this conversion, gaining oxygen and/or losing hydrogen.','primary alcohol':'The –OH-bearing carbon is attached to at most one other carbon.','ester':'A different organic family introduced in the next class.'},'Which organic product is formed in the simplified oxidation of ethanol used here?','Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH. Its complete –COOH group replaces the alcohol end of the two-carbon skeleton.','vinegar'),
 chapter('Compare, predict and explain before you investigate',[
 'Use the structure first. An alcohol in this lesson has –OH attached to a saturated carbon. A carboxylic acid has –C(=O)–OH. Both contain oxygen and hydrogen; the connections, not the presence of those letters alone, distinguish them.',
 'Use evidence second. Ethanoic acid is weak in water and reacts with a suitable base or carbonate. Ethanol is approximately neutral in the ordinary aqueous comparison and does not give the same acid–carbonate effervescence. Do not predict an exact pH or a reaction rate from a family name alone.',
 'On the practical work mat, choose ethanol and move its structural formula into position. Select the functional group and inspect its behaviour, then record the observation. Repeat with ethanoic acid and compare the two. Use Help with this step for a demonstration of one action. This is a molecular group model, not a measurement of reaction speed.',
 'The practice questions and Molecule Mission game below use ideas you have just studied. In the game, identify the whole functional group, sort the molecule, then explain why. The everyday-life rounds ask about fermentation, vinegar, carbon dioxide and the difference between weak and dilute. If unsure, return to the matching book chapter before trying again.'
 ],['Ask “What is bonded to the carbon beside O–H?”','If it is the carbonyl carbon in –COOH, identify a carboxylic acid; otherwise check the alcohol pattern taught here.','Explain your prediction using the complete group, then compare it with the practical model’s observation.'],'Structure → family → prediction → evidence → explanation.',{'prediction':'An expected result based on a model or idea.','evidence':'Observations used to assess a prediction.'},'What is the first thing to check when both candidate formulas contain OH?','Read the full connection around OH, especially whether C=O is attached to the same carbon.','comparison')
]

CHAPTERS.insert(2,chapter('Name an alcohol, step by step',[
 'A systematic name describes the structure. For the unbranched alcohols in this book, first choose the carbon chain containing the –OH-bearing carbon. Count its carbons: one gives meth-, two eth-, three prop- and four but-. These prefixes are not numbers of hydrogen atoms.',
 'Number the chain from the end that gives the –OH-bearing carbon the lowest position number. This position is a locant. Use the alcohol ending –ol. In longer simple names the position is placed before ol: propan-1-ol or propan-2-ol. A hyphen separates the number from the word parts. Methanol and ethanol need no position number because there is only one distinct position for OH on those chains.',
 'CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH is propan-1-ol: start numbering at the end nearest OH, so the OH carbon is carbon 1. CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃ is propan-2-ol: the OH is on the middle carbon, carbon 2. Brackets in CH(OH) show that the OH group is attached to that CH carbon. The two structures have the same total formula C₃H₈O but different connections; they are structural isomers.',
 'For CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–OH, choose four carbons and give OH position 1: butan-1-ol. Check the name by rebuilding the formula: four carbons with OH on carbon 1. At this introductory stage we use straight chains and one alcohol group. Branched chains and more than one OH require additional naming rules, introduced when you study those structures.'
 ],['Find the chain containing OH in CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH: three carbons → prop-.','Number from the OH end: the OH-bearing carbon is carbon 1.','Use the alcohol ending: propan-1-ol. Do not count from the other end and call the same unbranched structure propan-3-ol.'],'Count C → number nearest OH → use –ol. The locant identifies the OH-bearing carbon.',{'systematic name':'A name built from rules that describe a structure.','locant':'A position number in a chemical name.','structural isomers':'Molecules with the same molecular formula but different atom connections.'},'What is the name of CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃?','Propan-2-ol: three carbons, with OH attached to the middle carbon.'))
CHAPTERS.insert(5,chapter('Name a carboxylic acid, step by step',[
 'For the unbranched molecules with one carboxyl group shown here, choose the carbon chain that includes the carboxyl carbon. Count that carbon as carbon 1. The double-bonded O and the OH are not carbon atoms, so they do not add to the chain’s carbon count.',
 'Use the same carbon-count prefixes: meth- for one carbon, eth- for two, prop- for three and but- for four. The carboxylic-acid ending is –oic acid: methanoic acid, ethanoic acid, propanoic acid and butanoic acid. Keep the word acid in the name. In these simple single-carboxyl examples, a locant for COOH is unnecessary because its carbon is automatically carbon 1.',
 'H–C(=O)–OH is methanoic acid, also written HCOOH. CH₃–C(=O)–OH is ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH. CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–OH is propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH. CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–C(=O)–OH is butanoic acid. Read the complete connection, with O double-bonded above and OH below, rather than counting only the carbon group written on its left.',
 'Some familiar materials use common names: ethanoic acid is also called acetic acid, and methanoic acid is also called formic acid. These common names do not identify new compounds. In this class, practise the systematic names first. A salt has a different name: sodium ethanoate is not ethanoic acid, because it contains ethanoate ions rather than the unchanged acid molecule.'
 ],['Read CH₃–CH₂–COOH and count three carbons: one in CH₃, one in CH₂ and one in COOH.','Start at COOH if numbering is needed: its carbon is carbon 1.','Use prop- and the acid ending: propanoic acid. The common error is forgetting the carboxyl carbon and choosing ethanoic acid.'],'Count the COOH carbon too. One carboxyl group on these straight chains: –oic acid.',{'carboxyl carbon':'The carbon in the complete COOH group; carbon 1 for these acid chains.','common name':'A familiar alternative name, such as acetic acid for ethanoic acid.'},'What is the systematic name of CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–COOH?','Butanoic acid: four carbons in total, including the carboxyl carbon.'))

def picture(name,caption):
 return f'<figure class="book-figure"><img src="{ASSET}{name}.svg?v=1" alt="{E(caption,quote=True)}" width="1100" height="330"><figcaption>{E(caption)}</figcaption></figure>'
def gallery():
 examples=[('fuel','Ethanol as a fuel component','The same ethanol molecule can be part of a fuel blend or act as a solvent. Family names describe chemistry, not suitability for drinking.'),('fermentation','Yeast makes ethanol and carbon dioxide','In the simplified oxygen-poor fermentation model, glucose is rearranged into ethanol and CO₂. The gas can leave the fermenter.'),('vinegar','Vinegar contains dilute ethanoic acid','Vinegar is a solution in water. “Dilute” describes concentration; “weak” describes the acid’s partial ionisation.'),('fizz','Vinegar and baking soda form a gas','Ethanoic acid reacts with sodium hydrogencarbonate to form sodium ethanoate, water and carbon dioxide.')]
 return '<div class="book-example-gallery">'+''.join(f'<figure><img src="{ASSET}{kind}.svg" alt="{E(title)}" width="440" height="300"><figcaption><strong>{E(title)}</strong><p>{E(body)}</p></figcaption></figure>' for kind,title,body in examples)+'</div>'
def render_book():
 nav=''.join(f'<a href="#idea-{i}" data-book-page="{i-1}"><span>{i:02}</span>{E(c["title"])}</a>' for i,c in enumerate(CHAPTERS,1));pages=''
 for i,c in enumerate(CHAPTERS,1):
  art=''
  if i==3:art=picture('naming-alcohols','Choose the lowest OH position: propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are different structures.')
  elif i==6:art=picture('naming-acids','Number from the carboxyl carbon. Propanoic acid includes three carbons, not two.')
  elif c['picture']=='uses':art=gallery()
  elif c['picture']=='acids':art=picture('acids','Methanoic, ethanoic and propanoic acids: C=O rises steeply; OH descends from the same carbon. The carboxyl carbon is included in the carbon count.')
  elif c['picture']in('fizz','vinegar'):art=f'<figure class="book-figure book-small-picture"><img src="{ASSET}{c["picture"]}.svg" alt="Illustrated {c["picture"]} example" width="440" height="300"><figcaption>{"Acid–hydrogencarbonate effervescence produces CO₂." if c["picture"]=="fizz" else "Oxidation of ethanol can produce the ethanoic acid present in vinegar."}</figcaption></figure>'
  elif c['picture']=='comparison':art=picture('cover','Compare the alcohol –OH with the complete carboxyl group, –COOH. Drawings show connections; they are not scaled molecular photographs.')
  pages+=f'<article class="class-topic book-page" id="idea-{i}" data-book-chapter="{i-1}"><p class="book-page-label">CHAPTER {i:02} / {len(CHAPTERS):02}</p><h2 tabindex="-1">{E(c["title"])}</h2>'+''.join('<p class="book-paragraph">'+formula_html(p)+'</p>' for p in c['paragraphs'])+art+'<aside class="book-words"><h3>New words, explained</h3><dl>'+''.join('<dt>'+E(term)+'</dt><dd>'+formula_html(definition)+'</dd>' for term,definition in c['terms'].items())+'</dl></aside><aside class="class-worked"><h3>Worked example · one step at a time</h3><ol>'+''.join('<li>'+formula_html(step)+'</li>' for step in c['example'])+'</ol></aside><div class="class-memory"><b>Remember and explain why</b>'+formula_html(c['memory'])+'</div><details class="book-think"><summary>Pause and think · '+formula_html(c['think'])+'</summary><p>'+formula_html(c['answer'])+'</p></details></article>'
 return f'<section class="alcohol-book" id="class-reading" aria-label="Illustrated introductory book"><div class="book-heading"><p class="eyebrow">YOUR FIRST GUIDE TO THESE ORGANIC GROUPS</p><h2>Read it like a book. Build the idea, page by page.</h2><p>Start at Chapter 1 if this is new. Read the explanation, study the picture and work through the example. All ten chapters remain readable without JavaScript.</p></div><p class="functional-colour-key"><span class="fg-alcohol">Teal: alcohol –OH</span><span class="fg-acid">Coral: complete acid –COOH</span><span>Dark: the attached carbon chain. Colour is a reading aid, not the physical colour of an atom.</span></p><div class="book-layout"><aside class="book-sidebar"><h3>Contents</h3><nav aria-label="Book chapters">{nav}</nav><p id="book-reading-progress" role="status">10 illustrated chapters</p><a href="#workbench">After reading: practical model →</a><a href="#molecule-mission">Then try Molecule Mission →</a></aside><div class="book-pages">{pages}<nav class="book-page-controls" aria-label="Turn the book page" hidden><button type="button" data-book-turn="-1">← Previous chapter</button><span data-book-position>Chapter 1 of 10</span><button type="button" data-book-turn="1">Next chapter →</button></nav></div></div></section>'

def render_game():
 return '<section class="mission-section" id="molecule-mission"><p class="eyebrow">14 · APPLY THE IDEAS IN A GAME</p><h2>Molecule Mission: sort, name and explain.</h2><div class="mission-how"><h3>How to play</h3><ol><li><b>Read the structure.</b> For molecule rounds, click the whole functional group, then drag the molecule to Alcohol or Carboxylic acid. You can also select the card and choose a tray.</li><li><b>Explain or name it.</b> Choose the matching explanation. Naming rounds use the carbon-count and position rules taught in the book.</li><li><b>Connect the chemistry.</b> Solve everyday-life rounds using fermentation, vinegar and acid reactions. Check your reasoning, read the feedback and retry.</li></ol><p>3 stars on a first try, 2 on a second, 1 after more tries or with a hint. Revealed solutions earn 0. No timer. Practice and tricky-round retries are not recorded; choose Play for my teacher for a recorded new game when Google recording is confirmed.</p></div><div id="molecule-game-app"></div></section>'
